---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import conversions from '../data/conversions.json';
import tools from '../data/tools.json';
import filamentPresets from '../data/filamentPresets.json';
import site from '../data/site.json';

const uniqueFilamentTypes = [...new Set(filamentPresets.map(p => p.type))].length;
const conv28to32 = conversions.find(c => c.slug === '28mm-to-32mm');
---
<Layout
  title={`About STLACCESS | Built by a Warhammer & 3D Printing Hobbyist`}
  description={`Why I built STLACCESS to stop wasted filament and solve scale creep in tabletop 3D printing. Tested on a Bambu Lab A1 Mini with real PLA prints.`}
>
<div slot="masthead">
  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <nav class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 pt-12 pb-4 text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-slate-600" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">Home</a>
    <span class="mx-2 text-slate-800">/</span>
    <span class="text-slate-400" aria-current="page">About</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-20 text-center about-hero">
    <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-[#ff6b35] mb-4 block">By a hobbyist, for hobbyists</span>
    <h1 class="text-5xl md:text-7xl font-bold font-heading mb-6 tracking-tight">
      Stop guessing mini scale. <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">Stop wasting filament.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-slate-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      I built STLACCESS because I kept printing proxies that didn't match the rest of my army. Getting your STL files to table-ready scale shouldn't require guesswork or failed 10-hour prints.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-20 w-full">

  <!-- Story Content -->
  <article class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-16 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class="space-y-6" aria-labelledby="the-problem">
      <h2 id="the-problem" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">Why "32mm" rarely means 32mm</h2>
      <p>
        If you've been 3D printing tabletop miniatures for more than a week, you've experienced the frustration. You download an incredible proxy STL, slice it, hit print, and 8 hours later discover your new character towers over your Warhammer squad—or looks like a child standing next to your terrain.
      </p>
      <p>
        We call it <strong class="text-white">scale creep</strong>, but the root cause is sculptor inconsistency. Some creators measure to eye level, others to the crown of the head. Some account for base thickness, others don't. For anyone trying to build a cohesive army or gaming table, it leads to a pile of failed prints and wasted filament.
      </p>
      <p>
        I threw away enough mis-scaled models to get sick of the guesswork. So I broke out my <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">digital calipers</span> and started working out the actual math.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The Solution -->
    <section class="bg-slate-900/40 border border-white/5 p-8 md:p-12 rounded-[2.5rem] glow-box methodology-section" aria-labelledby="baseline-heading">
      <h2 id="baseline-heading" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading mb-6">A reliable baseline for every print</h2>
      <p class="mb-6">
        While "28mm" or "heroic scale" can mean whatever a sculptor wants it to mean, scale ratios like 1:56 are exact math. To fix the guesswork across different STL creators, I built these tools around a standard reference.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-8">
        I use a <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">175cm human baseline</span> as a consistent starting point. Different sculptors and game systems use different standards, but grounding every conversion on this baseline gives us a reliable bridge. Even if your preferred system measures slightly differently, the percentage ratio between scales stays mathematically solid.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="bg-black/40 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
          <h3 class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold mb-2">Eye level ({site.eyeLevelCm}cm)</h3>
          <p class="text-sm">
            The standard for fantasy and sci-fi miniatures (like Warhammer or D&D). Measures directly to eye level so helmets, hats, and spikes don't mess up the scale ratio.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-black/40 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
          <h3 class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold mb-2">Top of head ({site.baselineHeightCm}cm)</h3>
          <p class="text-sm">
            Common for historical wargaming, military kits, and display busts where exact proportion from boots to crown matters.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-8 p-6 rounded-2xl bg-white/[0.03] border border-white/5">
        <h4 class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#ff6b35] mb-3">Why this works</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
          Consistency is what prevents wasted print time. Converting a 28mm STL to 32mm takes a <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">{conv28to32.percentage}%</span> scale factor in your slicer. By keeping the baseline fixed, every model you scale using STLACCESS maintains the same proportions across your entire army.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How I Test -->
    <section class="space-y-6 verification-section" aria-labelledby="testing-heading">
      <h2 id="testing-heading" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">Tested on the build plate</h2>
      <p>
        Math on a screen is fine, but table-ready results are what actually matter. I validate scale calculations by printing test pieces and measuring them with <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">digital calipers</span>. Here is how real-world printing affects the numbers:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <div class="bg-slate-900/30 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-ruler text-[#ff6b35] text-lg mb-3 block"></i>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">FDM Calibration</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
            Slight over-extrusion can expand dimensions by <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">0.3% to 0.5%</span>. Keeping flow rate tuned is essential before measuring physical minis.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/30 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group text-[#ff6b35] text-lg mb-3 block"></i>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Layer Stepping</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
            Printing at <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">0.08mm</span> vs 0.2mm layer heights changes surface stepping, which can subtly shift crown-height caliper readings on rounded helmets.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/30 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-temperature-high text-[#ff6b35] text-lg mb-3 block"></i>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">Material Shrinkage</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
            PLA and PETG shrink roughly <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">0.2% to 0.5%</span> as they cool. I include material compensation options in the tools to counteract this.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        I test my scaling logic on my <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">{site.printerName}</span> primarily using PLA. If a converted proxy comes off the build plate looking out of proportion with the rest of the unit, I tweak the calculations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- The Journey -->
    <section class="space-y-6" aria-labelledby="the-journey">
      <h2 id="the-journey" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading">From print service stress to hobbyist builder</h2>
      <p>
        I started 3D printing back in <strong class="text-white">2017</strong> with a cheap entry-level FDM printer. Like a lot of people back then, I spent more time troubleshooting bed leveling, extrusion issues, and failed prints than actually printing models for the table.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2019, I tried turning the hobby into a business by running a 3D printing service in <strong class="text-white">Enschede, Netherlands</strong>. It didn't take long to realize that running a commercial print service completely drained the fun out of the hobby. The margins were tight, managing print queues felt like a grind, and I had no time left to print or paint minis for myself. Eventually, I shut down the service and stepped away.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I returned to 3D printing, I kept it strictly about the hobby. Now I print on a <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">{site.printerName}</span>—mostly PLA and PETG—focusing on Warhammer proxies, tabletop terrain, board game upgrades, and the occasional practical print. Zero commercial pressure, just enjoying the process.
      </p>
      <p>
        Running that print service taught me one key lesson: nobody wants to commit to a 10-hour print only to find out the model is scaled wrong. I built STLACCESS to solve the exact scaling headaches I kept hitting on my own workbench.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What This Is -->
    <section class="bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5" aria-labelledby="what-is-heading">
      <h2 id="what-is-heading" class="text-3xl font-bold text-white font-heading mb-6">What STLACCESS is (and isn't)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-slate-400">
        <p>
          <strong class="text-white">Built for the painting desk.</strong> I don't sell STL files, spools of filament, or printers. There are no paywalls or affiliate tricks. I built these calculators for my own workflow and published them so other tabletop printers can save time and filament.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-white">Grounded in real printing experience.</strong> The numbers aren't just theoretical math. They are tested with physical test prints, measured with calipers, and adjusted when real-world results don't look tabletop-ready.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-white">Driven by community input.</strong> New tabletop systems and STL sculptors appear constantly. If a conversion percentage looks slightly off for your army on the build plate, let me know. I regularly test feedback and update the scale library.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Author Stats -->
    <section class="bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 text-center" aria-labelledby="stats-heading">
      <h2 id="stats-heading" class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#ff6b35] mb-6">By the numbers</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-8 max-w-lg mx-auto">
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white font-heading">{conversions.length}+</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">Scale conversions</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white font-heading">{tools.length}</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">Free browser tools</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white font-heading">{uniqueFilamentTypes}</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-1">Material profiles</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-6">
        <a href={site.author.url} class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-code-branch text-xs"></i>
          Follow on GitHub
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

  </article>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <div class="mt-24 pt-12 border-t border-white/5 text-center">
    <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-6">Get your scale right before you print</h3>
    <a
      href="/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-3 bg-[#ff6b35] text-black px-10 py-5 rounded-2xl font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest hover:scale-105 hover:shadow-2xl hover:shadow-[#ff6b35]/30 active:scale-95 transition-all duration-200"
    >
      <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator"></i>
      Launch Scale Calculator
    </a>
  </div>
</div>
</Layout>